{
  "an10.51:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.51 ",
  "an10.51:0.2": "6. Your Own Mind ",
  "an10.51:0.3": "Your Own Mind ",
  "an10.51:1.1": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "an10.51:1.2": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, ",
  "an10.51:1.3": "“Mendicants!” ",
  "an10.51:1.4": "“Venerable sir,” they replied. ",
  "an10.51:1.5": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "an10.51:2.1": "“Mendicants, if a mendicant isn’t skilled in encompassing another’s mind, then they should train themselves: ‘I will be skilled in encompassing my own mind.’ ",
  "an10.51:2.2": "",
  "an10.51:3.1": "And how is a mendicant skilled in encompassing their own mind? ",
  "an10.51:3.2": "Suppose there was a woman or man who was young, youthful, and fond of adornments, and they check their own reflection in a clean bright mirror or a clear bowl of water. If they see any dirt or blemish there, they’d try to remove it. ",
  "an10.51:3.3": "But if they don’t see any dirt or blemish there, they’re happy with that, as they’ve got all they wished for: ",
  "an10.51:3.4": "‘How fortunate that I’m clean!’ ",
  "an10.51:3.5": "In the same way, checking is very helpful for a mendicant’s skillful qualities. ",
  "an10.51:3.6": "‘Am I often covetous or not? Am I often malicious or not? Am I often overcome with dullness and drowsiness or not? Am I often restless or not? Am I often doubtful or not? Am I often irritable or not? Am I often corrupt